# A Glimpse into Hong Kong University Libraries

Endless queues for the morning elevators, strangers sitting shoulder-to-shoulder at loud canteen tables, and students silently fighting for a single library chair—these are stereotypes of universities in Hong Kong, and to some degree, they are true. One prominent “victim” of this confined space is the library. However, compared to American regional state universities, some Hong Kong university libraries even possess larger spaces. It may seem to be a paradox: how could a large space be so cramped? One obvious reason for this is the large student base, resulting in three times the capacity pressure of a regular American university.

Universities in Hong Kong have implemented strict rules to regulate visitor flow and maximize space utility. Some measures, including limiting the access of ordinary citizens to prevent them from crowding out students' resources and curbing seat hogging, did alleviate the problem to some degree, yet never resolved it.

Despite the gigantic student base, it is still noticeable that some universities in other areas or countries do not confront a lack of library space for students, even if they have similar student numbers and library sizes. This is somehow still attributable to space, but in other facilities. For example, an American student can find empty space in dorms, vacant classrooms, and student centers, while students in Hong Kong can hardly find any vacant space except libraries to complete their assignments and self-learning.

# Understanding U.S. Campus Shootings

Getting shot by a gun sounds terrible; what’s worse is hearing others being wounded all the time . On social media and in public awareness, U.S. school shootings are perceived as quite frequent. In fact, there have been only 17 mass shooting cases at universities since the 1960s. The public's perception derives mostly from three reasons. Firstly, there is confusion with K-12 (high school, middle school, primary school, and kindergarten) data. Many mass shootings that happen in these schools are lumped into the broad category of "school shootings," while universities are actually very secure. Secondly, social media and news coverage also amplify the frequency and severity of school shootings. Finally, many school shootings cannot be counted as mass shootings since they are aimed at specific person or are simply suicides.

To avoid any potential threats, most universities have their own safety systems, including apps, mass notifications, and Blue Light Emergency Phones on campus. Students will immediately receive notifications on their phones when a school shooting happens, and they can also use the app to request help. If students accidentally lose their phones or are being chased, they can press the red button on a Blue Light Tower, which will notify campus police and flash blue lights repeatedly.

One interesting fact is that the app will not only report emergency situations on campus, but also shootings a few blocks away. So, it is quite frequent to receive warnings or alerts.

Anyway, I hope everyone who reads this article will never have to face any of these emergency situations.


# When New Science Explaining the Unknown：From Spirit to Griefbots

As the patient’s very last breath stopped, one end of the balance suddenly smashed on the ground. Dr. MacDougall eagerly looked at the scale—21 grams—the famous weight of the soul was now being measured.

Duncan MacDougall was not an occultist, nor a zealot, but a professional doctor in the 19th century. He was only one of the numerous intellectuals who believed in Spiritualism, specifically, Scientific Spiritualism.

It may seem a little bit perplexing: how could spiritualism be interwoven with science? But think about one single example—the well-known Hollywood movie, *Ghostbusters*. The “ecto goggles” (special glasses for seeing invisible spirits) in the movie best exemplify some scientists’ ideas at that time—that spiritualism is not a scam or fake, but needs new explanations for the mechanisms behind these paranormal phenomena. Many scientists in physics, chemistry, and psychology proposed various theories, which used cutting-edge scientific discoveries as evidence. Brain-Wave Theory was one of them, which provided some inspiration for the later ITC theory.

> Side note of ITC
>
> Instrumental Transcommunication (ITC) suggests that spirits communicate through electronic devices. Its subset, Electronic Voice Phenomenon (EVP), involves capturing alleged ghostly voices on audio recordings.In 1959, filmmaker Friedrich Jürgenson was recording bird songs on magnetic tape, only to discover his deceased mother's voice hidden in the static. This is the origin of EVP.

While the ITC theory and other theories in Scientific Spiritualism are clearly ridiculous in our view, they may actually have been subjects of serious scientific discussion in that era. However, the public extracted mostly the compelling, eye-catching content, using simplified versions to explain everyday phenomena or events—mostly through analogy, which is an inaccurate tool whose functions include pursuing aesthetic value instead of rigor. In this case, properties of  physics terms such as “wave,” “resonance,” and “aether”—transmitting information, omnipresent—were linked with ghosts’ ability to travel through walls and justified the plausibility of their existence.

From the vantage point of history, most of us will not take Scientific Spiritualism and the corresponding derivative public perception of spirits as “correct.” While the advancement of science continues, new concerns and issues emerge. The most discussed and debated field is artificial intelligence——deadbots or griefbots (AI-powered digital avatars which imitate the departed), for example, are raising attention from government institutions, AI companies, the scientific community, and the public. For instance, 67% of American residents consider AI to have “some level of consciousness,” according to a survey conducted by University College London and the University of Waterloo. Yet, immediately guarding against or even resisting this potential trend could be an overreaction. A social consensus is not achieved overnight. The lateral development and decline of Scientific Spiritualism gives us inspiration about this—new scientific advancements in multiple areas, especially physics and psychology, presented vital defects in Scientific Spiritualism, leading to its demise. A better way to handle these phenomena is to maintain an open mindset. Theories and evidence emerge and fall in such a rapidly developing area. In the sweep of history, our path forward is uncharted, and no definite judgements can be made. When we look back from the future, we will see how we tamed and explained “ghosts” in our times.
